"""Twenty further works, written as twelve distinct spoken storybook beats."""
def register(add):
    def A(title,author,year,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,question,answer,source,quote,context,meaning,note=''):
        url=f'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/{source}' if isinstance(source,int) else source
        rows=[]
        for line in frames.strip().splitlines():
            heading,text,people,motif=line.split('|')
            rows.append('~'.join([heading,text,people,motif,heading.upper()]))
        add(title,author,year,str(year),era,genre,setting,hook,cast,'\n'.join(rows),question,answer,source=url,note=note)
        # The caller returns its newly registered dictionary.
        return dict(text=quote,context=context,meaning=meaning,source=url)
    def book(*args,**kwargs):
        q=A(*args,**kwargs)
        add.last['quotes']=[q]
        add.last['spoken']=True

    book('Twelfth Night','William Shakespeare',1601,'renaissance','Comedy','Illyria','One shipwreck, two twins—and love following the wrong name!','Viola=Shipwrecked twin disguised as Cesario|Sebastian=Her surviving brother|Olivia=Countess who falls for Cesario|Orsino=Duke courting Olivia|Malvolio=Steward deceived by a letter|Feste=Sharp-eyed fool', '''
The sea separates them|A shipwreck tears Viola from Sebastian! She reaches Illyria believing her brother lost. How can she begin again in this unfamiliar country?|Viola|sea
A borrowed identity|Viola becomes Cesario and enters Orsino's service. The disguise offers protection—but now every friendship must pass through a name that is not hers!|Viola,Orsino|mask
The messenger falls in love|Orsino sends Cesario to woo Olivia. Viola obeys while falling for Orsino herself! Can she speak for another lover without revealing her own heart?|Viola,Orsino|heart
Olivia looks at the messenger|Olivia refuses the duke, then finds herself drawn to Cesario! The message has missed its destination, and the messenger has become the object of desire.|Viola,Olivia|letter
A ring creates a problem|Olivia sends a ring after Cesario. Viola recognises what has happened: her disguise has made a triangle of wishes that cannot all be answered!|Viola,Olivia|coins
The steward dreams of greatness|Malvolio imagines marrying Olivia. Maria's forged letter seems to encourage him—what a perfect trap for someone already persuaded by his own ambition!|Malvolio|letter
Yellow stockings and confusion|Following the letter, Malvolio smiles and wears yellow stockings. Olivia sees alarming behaviour where he expects admiration! The joke begins to grow cruel.|Malvolio,Olivia|cloth
The missing twin arrives|Sebastian is alive! He enters a town that keeps mistaking him for Cesario, and people offer quarrels and affection he has never earned.|Sebastian|road
One face and two lives|Olivia marries Sebastian, believing him Cesario. Meanwhile Viola faces accusations she cannot explain! What happens when a disguise acquires another person's real body?|Viola,Sebastian,Olivia|mirror
The twins stand together|At last Viola and Sebastian meet! Their shared appearance explains the impossible encounters, turning apparent betrayal into recognition and recovering the family lost at sea.|Viola,Sebastian|heart
Love rearranges itself|Orsino learns who Viola is and turns toward her. Olivia keeps Sebastian! The confusions settle into marriages, though some wounds remain outside the celebration.|Viola,Orsino,Olivia|garden
The song outlasts the party|Malvolio leaves promising revenge. Feste sings of rain and ordinary life! Remember both endings: joyful recognition, and the hurt the comedy cannot simply laugh away.|Malvolio,Feste|storm''','Why does Sebastian resolve the confusion?','He is Viola’s twin, so the supposed contradictions concern two people with the same appearance.',1526,'Some are born great','Malvolio reads the forged letter, Act II, scene 5.','A tempting promise becomes a trap because it flatters the reader’s existing ambition.','Approximate first performance around 1601–1602; date is a timeline anchor.')
    book('The Merchant of Venice','William Shakespeare',1596,'renaissance','Problem comedy','Venice and Belmont','A promise becomes a knife—and mercy enters the courtroom!','Antonio=Merchant guaranteeing a loan|Bassanio=Friend seeking to marry Portia|Portia=Heiress who later argues in court|Shylock=Jewish moneylender subjected to hostility|Jessica=Shylock’s daughter|Nerissa=Portia’s companion', '''
A friend needs money|Bassanio wants to court Portia, but needs funds. Antonio's wealth is tied up in ships! What can a generous promise cost when the money is elsewhere?|Antonio,Bassanio|ship
The unusual bond|Shylock lends the money on a startling condition: a pound of Antonio's flesh if repayment fails! Their hostility makes this agreement far more dangerous than a joke.|Shylock,Antonio|contract
Belmont holds another test|Portia's father's will requires suitors to choose among three caskets. Gold, silver, lead—which outward appearance can guide someone toward the right marriage?|Portia|coins
Jessica leaves her father|Jessica elopes with Lorenzo and takes money and jewels. Shylock suffers loss amid public hostility! The household's fracture intensifies his bitterness.|Jessica,Shylock|gate
The ships seem lost|News reaches Venice that Antonio's ships have failed. The repayment deadline approaches! Suddenly the terrible condition becomes a threat to an actual life.|Antonio,Shylock|storm
Lead wins the choice|Bassanio chooses lead and wins Portia. She gives him a ring with a promise attached! Remember the modest casket beside the bond obsessed with literal payment.|Bassanio,Portia|heart
The happy couple hear bad news|Antonio's danger reaches Belmont. Bassanio rushes back, while Portia prepares her own intervention! Can the marriage's new resources reach the threatened friend in time?|Bassanio,Portia,Antonio|letter
The court awaits an answer|Shylock insists on his bond despite offers of money. Antonio prepares for death. The room needs more than generosity now—it needs an argument that can stop the knife!|Shylock,Antonio|sword
Mercy is requested|Disguised as a legal expert, Portia urges mercy. Shylock refuses! Her appeal raises a question that the play's Christians must also answer about their own conduct.|Portia,Shylock|book
The words turn against the bond|Portia permits flesh but no blood. The contract cannot be fulfilled safely! Laws then turn against Shylock, whose compelled conversion complicates the supposed victory.|Portia,Shylock,Antonio|contract
The ring tests a different promise|Portia asks Bassanio for his ring as payment. He gives it away! Back at Belmont, an apparently small object reveals another gap between promise and action.|Portia,Bassanio,Nerissa|coins
News returns to Belmont|The disguises are revealed and Antonio hears some ships are safe. Celebration returns—but keep Shylock's punishment in view! Whose pain has paid for this comic ending?|Portia,Antonio,Shylock|ship''','How does Portia stop the execution of the bond?','She argues that the bond permits flesh but gives no right to shed blood.',1515,'The quality of mercy is not strain’d,','Portia, Act IV, scene 1.','The appeal to mercy must be considered beside the coercion inflicted after the trial.','Approximate composition c. 1596–1597. The play depicts antisemitism; Shylock is not a comic racial stereotype to imitate.')
    book('As You Like It','William Shakespeare',1599,'renaissance','Pastoral comedy','The court and the Forest of Arden','Into the forest—with a disguise and a lesson in love!','Rosalind=Exiled daughter disguised as Ganymede|Orlando=Young man in love with Rosalind|Celia=Rosalind’s loyal cousin|Jaques=Melancholy observer|Touchstone=Court fool|Oliver=Orlando’s hostile brother', '''
The court turns hostile|Orlando suffers his brother Oliver's hostility, while Rosalind lives under an uneasy ruler. Before the forest's freedom, notice how power makes both young people vulnerable!|Orlando,Rosalind,Oliver|crown
A wrestling match changes everything|Orlando wins a dangerous wrestling match and meets Rosalind. Attraction arrives quickly! A small exchange gives their future forest encounters a remembered beginning.|Orlando,Rosalind|heart
Exile demands a decision|Duke Frederick banishes Rosalind. Celia refuses to abandon her cousin! Friendship becomes action, and leaving the court means creating a different way to travel safely.|Rosalind,Celia|gate
Ganymede enters the road|Rosalind dresses as Ganymede, Celia takes another name, and Touchstone joins them. What adventures can a borrowed identity permit—and what truths will it complicate?|Rosalind,Celia,Touchstone|mask
Arden offers another world|They reach the forest, where an exiled duke lives among companions. Pastoral freedom is attractive, but food, property and ordinary needs still matter!|Rosalind,Celia|forest
Love covers the trees|Orlando hangs verses for Rosalind through Arden. She discovers them while dressed as someone else! How can she answer the lover who does not recognise his beloved?|Rosalind,Orlando|letter
The lover meets his teacher|Ganymede offers to cure Orlando's love through practice. Rosalind can now test his promises! Their playful rehearsals bring fantasy into contact with actual conversation.|Rosalind,Orlando|heart
Other lovers join the puzzle|Shepherds and shepherdesses struggle with affection, including Phoebe's attraction to Ganymede. The forest multiplies the questions! A disguise that teaches one lover misleads another.|Rosalind|village
Jaques watches the performance|Jaques offers his famous account of life's stages. Beside the romantic bustle, his melancholy reminds us that people also play roles beyond the current comedy!|Jaques|stage
Orlando acts rather than boasts|Orlando saves Oliver from danger. The brother's hostility gives way to gratitude! Here is a different love lesson: deeds can change a relationship that words alone cannot heal.|Orlando,Oliver|forest
A promise gathers the couples|Rosalind arranges a meeting and promises to resolve the entanglements. Everyone waits! The person managing the disguise is also preparing the moment when it must end.|Rosalind,Orlando,Celia|contract
The forest gives them back|Rosalind reveals herself and marriages follow. Political news promises restoration, while Jaques chooses another path. The forest changes them—but not everyone wants the same happy ending!|Rosalind,Orlando,Jaques|garden''','What does Rosalind gain from the Ganymede disguise?','She can question and educate Orlando’s romantic expectations before revealing herself.',1523,"All the world's a stage,",'Jaques, Act II, scene 7.','The forest’s disguises sit within a wider reflection on the roles people perform.','Approximate composition date.')
    book('Persuasion','Jane Austen',1817,'romantic','Novel of second chances','Somerset, Lyme and Bath','Eight years of silence—then the man she lost walks back in!','Anne Elliot=Woman who once refused Wentworth|Captain Wentworth=Returned naval officer|Lady Russell=Adviser who opposed the match|Louisa Musgrove=Young woman whose accident changes the group|Sir Walter Elliot=Anne’s status-conscious father', '''
The house cannot afford its pride|Sir Walter's debts force the Elliots to let their home. Anne watches appearances consume practical judgement! The departure will also bring her past dangerously close.|Anne Elliot,Sir Walter Elliot|home
The old refusal returns|Years earlier Anne broke her engagement to Wentworth under advice. He has now gained wealth and distinction! Could the very objections that separated them have disappeared?|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|letter
He enters the room again|Wentworth returns through naval connections. Anne still loves him, but his coolness stings! The reunion offers no easy permission to begin where they stopped.|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|heart
Small kindness matters|Anne quietly helps others while brighter company draws attention. Do not mistake her restraint for emptiness! Her worth becomes visible through steady care rather than fashionable display.|Anne Elliot|home
Louisa appears certain|Wentworth admires Louisa's firmness, which seems unlike Anne's former hesitation. But is unbending determination always wisdom? His preference will meet a frightening test.|Captain Wentworth,Louisa Musgrove|contract
The walk to Lyme changes the story|At Lyme, the friends explore the seaside. An ordinary outing holds unsuspected danger! Anne's composure will soon matter more than the social hierarchy around her.|Anne Elliot,Louisa Musgrove|sea
One leap ends the merriment|Louisa falls during a reckless jump and is badly injured. Panic spreads! Anne organises help, showing the practical strength that the others have too easily overlooked.|Anne Elliot,Louisa Musgrove,Captain Wentworth|stone
The certainty must be reconsidered|Wentworth sees that Anne's judgement has depth. Louisa's recovery brings another attachment! The supposed romantic obstacle moves, while his understanding of Anne begins to change.|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|mirror
Bath revives the pressure|Anne returns to a world of rank and social manoeuvring. A flattering suitor appears! Yet old family ambitions cannot decide where her affection belongs.|Anne Elliot,Sir Walter Elliot|city
A conversation reaches a listener|Anne debates whether women love faithfully through absence. Wentworth hears her! What ordinary conversation can say openly, her guarded encounters with him have not dared to say.|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|book
The letter breaks the silence|Wentworth writes while listening and gives Anne the letter. At last feeling becomes direct speech! Eight years of uncertainty concentrate into a chance she can choose for herself.|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|letter
They choose again|Anne and Wentworth reunite with deeper understanding. The future still includes naval uncertainty, but her decision is her own! Remember this ending as renewed judgement, not erased history.|Anne Elliot,Captain Wentworth|ship''','What changes Anne’s position at the end?','Experience strengthens her independent judgement, and Wentworth recognises the constancy he had misread.',105,'You pierce my soul.','Wentworth’s letter, Chapter 23.','The letter turns overheard feeling into an unmistakable declaration.','Published posthumously in December 1817 with a title page dated 1818.')
    book('A Christmas Carol','Charles Dickens',1843,'victorian','Ghost novella','London on Christmas Eve and Christmas Day','Three visitors, one frozen heart—and a morning still worth changing!','Ebenezer Scrooge=Isolated miser given a chance to change|Bob Cratchit=Underpaid clerk and devoted father|Tiny Tim=Bob’s vulnerable son|Jacob Marley=Dead partner bearing chains|The Christmas spirits=Guides through past present and future', '''
The office has no warmth|Scrooge guards money while Bob shivers at work. Christmas kindness irritates him! Meet a man who has made his own comfort depend on refusing other people's needs.|Ebenezer Scrooge,Bob Cratchit|coins
The dead partner knocks|Marley's ghost appears wearing chains. These are the burdens of a life spent neglecting others! He warns Scrooge that the same future can still be changed.|Ebenezer Scrooge,Jacob Marley|chains
The first spirit opens a door|The Ghost of Christmas Past leads Scrooge into memory. He sees his lonely younger self! Beneath the hard present lies a child whose pain never received enough care.|Ebenezer Scrooge,The Christmas spirits|door
A generous employer is remembered|Fezziwig's celebration glows with fellowship. Scrooge remembers what an employer's kindness can mean! Money matters here because someone uses it to make others welcome.|Ebenezer Scrooge|hall
Love names the change|Belle recognises that money has displaced their attachment. Scrooge watches the relationship he sacrificed! His present loneliness begins to look less inevitable than chosen.|Ebenezer Scrooge|heart
The second spirit fills the room|Christmas Present arrives with abundance and cheer! Scrooge moves outward from private memory toward the lives continuing around him, lives he has never bothered to understand.|Ebenezer Scrooge,The Christmas spirits|garden
The Cratchits share their small feast|Bob's family makes warmth from limited means. Tiny Tim's vulnerability catches Scrooge's attention! Can someone still dismiss poverty after seeing the people inside it?|Bob Cratchit,Tiny Tim,Ebenezer Scrooge|home
Two children reveal the warning|The spirit exposes Ignorance and Want. The festive journey turns urgent! These neglected children challenge a society that congratulates itself while refusing responsibility.|Ebenezer Scrooge,The Christmas spirits|storm
The silent visitor approaches|Christmas Yet to Come offers no comforting explanation. Scrooge must read the scenes himself! A neglected death and casual indifference lead him toward a terrifying recognition.|Ebenezer Scrooge,The Christmas spirits|ghost
The name lies on the stone|Scrooge sees his own grave and hears of Tiny Tim's death. He begs for another chance! Are these fixed events, or warnings about the road he continues to choose?|Ebenezer Scrooge,Tiny Tim|grave
Morning arrives alive|He wakes on Christmas Day—there is still time! Scrooge sends food, gives generously and seeks company. The change begins through actions others can actually feel.|Ebenezer Scrooge|sunrise
The heart becomes a habit|Scrooge supports Bob and Tiny Tim and keeps his promise beyond one morning. Remember the real miracle! A frightened revelation matters because a different life follows it.|Ebenezer Scrooge,Bob Cratchit,Tiny Tim|heart''','What makes Scrooge’s transformation more than a feeling?','His continuing generosity and care change the lives of the Cratchits and his own relationships.',46,'Mankind was my business.','Marley, Stave One.','The ghost names the human responsibility his living business had ignored.')
    book('The Mayor of Casterbridge','Thomas Hardy',1886,'victorian','Tragic novel','Casterbridge in Wessex','A terrible bargain follows a man all the way to the mayor’s chair!','Michael Henchard=Mayor haunted by his past|Susan=Woman he sold at a fair|Elizabeth-Jane=Young woman he hopes to retain|Donald Farfrae=Newcomer whose gifts threaten him|Lucetta=Former lover whose past becomes public', '''
The fair makes a dreadful bargain|Drunk and angry, Henchard sells Susan and her child to a sailor. A family becomes a transaction! This appalling act will refuse to stay in the past.|Michael Henchard,Susan|coins
A vow follows shame|Sober, Henchard searches without success and swears to avoid alcohol for twenty-one years. He can change a habit—but can a vow undo the people he has harmed?|Michael Henchard|contract
The town knows another man|Years later he is Casterbridge's successful mayor. Susan returns with Elizabeth-Jane! Respectability now has to face a history that the town has never heard.|Michael Henchard,Susan,Elizabeth-Jane|city
The marriage is quietly repaired|Henchard remarries Susan while hiding the earlier sale. His plan restores an outward household! Yet secrecy makes the attempted repair depend upon another unstable appearance.|Michael Henchard,Susan|home
A newcomer brings bright ideas|Farfrae offers practical help and wins goodwill. Henchard first welcomes him! But another man's competence soon threatens the mayor's need to remain the central figure.|Michael Henchard,Donald Farfrae|coins
Friendship hardens into rivalry|Business competition and jealousy turn Henchard against Farfrae. Watch the old impulsiveness return! The successful public man still struggles to govern his own reactions.|Michael Henchard,Donald Farfrae|storm
Susan leaves a sealed truth|After Susan dies, a letter reveals that Elizabeth-Jane is not Henchard's daughter. His treatment of her cools! The wish for possession overrides the care she deserves.|Michael Henchard,Elizabeth-Jane|letter
Lucetta chooses a different future|Lucetta's history with Henchard becomes tangled with her love for Farfrae. She marries the newcomer! Henchard must face another relationship he cannot command back into place.|Michael Henchard,Lucetta,Donald Farfrae|heart
The crowd makes private shame public|A cruel procession exposes Lucetta's past. The shock contributes to her death! The town's merriment carries a human cost that gossip prefers not to see.|Lucetta|mask
The former mayor loses his footing|The old sale is revealed, business fails, and Henchard loses status. Alcohol returns after the vow's term! His public fall exposes the loneliness behind his pride.|Michael Henchard|grave
Another father reaches the door|The sailor Newson returns for Elizabeth-Jane. Henchard lies that she is dead! Fear of abandonment tempts him to destroy the very trust that could keep her near.|Michael Henchard,Elizabeth-Jane|gate
The final wish erases a name|Elizabeth-Jane discovers the lie; Henchard withdraws and dies alone. His will asks to be forgotten. Remember how a need to possess love can end by losing it!|Michael Henchard,Elizabeth-Jane|book''','Why does Henchard lie to Newson?','He fears losing Elizabeth-Jane, but his attempt to keep her destroys her trust.',143,'Happiness was but the occasional episode in a general drama of pain.','Elizabeth-Jane’s perspective near the end.','Her patient view of life contrasts with Henchard’s impulsive pursuit of security.')
    book('Jude the Obscure','Thomas Hardy',1895,'victorian','Tragic social novel','Rural Wessex and Christminster','A boy sees distant towers—and discovers the doors are not open to everyone!','Jude Fawley=Working-class aspirant to scholarship|Sue Bridehead=His intellectually independent cousin|Arabella Donn=Jude’s first wife|Richard Phillotson=Teacher and Sue’s husband|Little Father Time=Jude’s son', '''
The distant city shines|Young Jude dreams of Christminster and learning. The towers seem to promise another life! He begins with books, hope and very little practical power.|Jude Fawley|castle
He studies after work|Jude teaches himself while labouring. Knowledge is hard won! Yet enthusiasm and effort alone cannot remove the money and class barriers guarding institutional education.|Jude Fawley|book
Arabella changes the road|Jude marries Arabella under misleading circumstances. Their mismatch soon becomes painful! His imagined scholarly future now competes with obligations made before he understood the relationship.|Jude Fawley,Arabella Donn|contract
The city refuses its admirer|He reaches Christminster and works as a stonemason, but admission remains out of reach. How cruel! He can help maintain the buildings without entering the life they represent.|Jude Fawley|stone
Sue enters his imagination|Jude meets his cousin Sue and feels a powerful bond. Her questioning mind draws him! Affection becomes another hope that existing rules will make difficult.|Jude Fawley,Sue Bridehead|heart
An unhappy marriage divides them|Sue marries Phillotson but feels trapped. Her wish to leave challenges accepted marital authority! Phillotson eventually releases her, paying a heavy social price for allowing choice.|Sue Bridehead,Richard Phillotson|gate
They attempt another household|Jude and Sue live together outside conventional marriage. Their attachment seeks freedom! But landlords, neighbours and employers can turn moral judgement into material insecurity.|Jude Fawley,Sue Bridehead|home
A child hears the burden|Little Father Time joins the family, whose poverty and uncertain shelter press upon everyone. Watch his anxious seriousness! Adult hardship is entering a child's understanding.|Jude Fawley,Little Father Time|storm
The household meets catastrophe|The boy kills the younger children and himself, interpreting existence as a burden. The horror breaks the family's hopes. How can the survivors understand such a loss?|Sue Bridehead,Little Father Time,Jude Fawley|grave
Sue seeks punishment in duty|Shattered by grief, Sue returns to Phillotson and rejects the freedom she once pursued. Belief has changed under trauma! Her return cannot simply be read as happy reconciliation.|Sue Bridehead,Richard Phillotson|chains
Jude returns to an empty promise|Jude remarries Arabella while illness and disappointment deepen. Christminster's celebrations continue nearby! The city's brightness cannot answer the exclusion that shaped his life.|Jude Fawley,Arabella Donn|city
The towers remain after him|Jude dies with his aspirations unfulfilled. Hold the distant city in mind! The novel asks what institutions and social judgement destroy when they refuse vulnerable people room to live.|Jude Fawley|castle''','How do social rules create practical hardship?','Marriage expectations, class barriers and moral stigma restrict education, housing, employment and the family’s choices.',153,'Done because we are too menny.','Little Father Time’s note, Part Sixth, Chapter II; original spelling.','The terrible line shows a child internalising the household’s insecurity.','Serialised 1894–1895; book publication 1895. Scenes discuss child deaths without graphic illustration.')
    book('The Time Machine','H. G. Wells',1895,'victorian','Scientific romance','Victorian England and Earth’s distant future','Pull the lever! The future looks gentle—until darkness reveals who keeps it running!','The Time Traveller=Inventor exploring the future|Weena=Eloi woman he befriends|The Eloi=Surface-dwelling people|The Morlocks=Underground people|The narrator=Listener preserving the account', '''
The dinner becomes an experiment|The Time Traveller proposes travel through time and demonstrates a model. His guests doubt him! Can an impossible idea survive a room full of confident objections?|The Time Traveller,The narrator|laboratory
The lever moves|He tests the full-sized machine. Days rush past! The familiar world becomes changing light, and a journey through time replaces the comfort of looking at a calendar.|The Time Traveller|clock
A beautiful future appears|He stops in the year 802,701 among the small Eloi. Their gentle surroundings seem inviting! But why do such apparently carefree people show so little curiosity?|The Time Traveller,The Eloi|garden
The machine disappears|The Time Traveller finds his machine missing. Wonder turns into fear! Without it, the lovely future could become a prison with no road back home.|The Time Traveller|gate
Weena offers a bond|He rescues Weena from drowning, and she becomes attached to him. Their friendship warms the unfamiliar world! Yet care does not supply a full explanation of its dangers.|The Time Traveller,Weena|river
The dark has inhabitants|Pale Morlocks emerge through wells and underground passages. Something lives beneath the garden! The future has two populations, and their relationship is not yet clear.|The Time Traveller,The Morlocks|cave
The social theory changes|He imagines class divisions separating humanity into surface leisure and underground labour. The picture is unsettling! Present inequality may have grown into a biological and social split.|The Time Traveller,The Eloi,The Morlocks|chains
The night reveals the appetite|The Morlocks prey upon the Eloi. Apparent tranquillity collapses! The beautiful surface depends upon a relationship far more dreadful than the traveller's first explanation.|The Eloi,The Morlocks|ghost
The museum offers old tools|He explores an abandoned museum and gathers materials. Knowledge survives as debris! The familiar human past becomes a resource inside a future that has forgotten its purposes.|The Time Traveller,Weena|book
Fire becomes a desperate defence|In the forest, fire spreads during an encounter with Morlocks. Weena is lost! The traveller's attempt at protection brings escape but cannot guarantee the companion's safety.|The Time Traveller,Weena,The Morlocks|fire
The last Earth lies quiet|Recovering the machine, he travels farther to a dying world beneath a red sun. Beyond human rivalry waits another ending! Progress has no promise of continuing forever.|The Time Traveller|sunset
The account leaves flowers behind|He returns, tells the story, then departs again and vanishes. The narrator keeps Weena's flowers! A fragile sign of tenderness survives where certainty about the journey does not.|The narrator,Weena|garden''','Why does the first impression of the Eloi prove misleading?','Surface ease conceals predation and a divided social order maintained beneath it.',35,'We are always getting away from the present moment.','The Time Traveller, opening discussion.','The claim invites experiment, while the journey exposes change’s frightening possibilities.')
    book('Animal Farm','George Orwell',1945,'postwar','Political fable','A farm in an allegorical England','The animals overthrow the farmer—but who will watch the new rulers?','Napoleon=Pig consolidating power|Snowball=Pig proposing plans before his expulsion|Boxer=Trusting and hardworking horse|Squealer=Pig manipulating explanations|Benjamin=Sceptical donkey|Old Major=Boar whose speech inspires rebellion', '''
The barn hears a promise|Old Major describes a life without human exploitation. The animals listen with hope! A shared vision begins the rebellion, but inspiration alone cannot decide how power will be used.|Old Major,Boxer|hall
The farmer is driven out|Hunger and mistreatment provoke revolt. Jones loses the farm! For a moment the animals believe their labour and food can belong to everyone who produces them.|Boxer,Napoleon,Snowball|gate
The rules go on the wall|The pigs write commandments of equality. The animals begin organising work! Yet literacy is uneven, and the ability to explain the rules already gives some voices greater authority.|Squealer,Boxer|book
Milk and apples vanish upward|The pigs reserve privileges and justify them as necessary brain work. Listen to the explanation! A small exception can teach a community to accept the next, larger exception.|Squealer,Napoleon|apple
The heroes meet an enemy|The animals repel a human attack. Snowball helps lead the defence! Collective victory could strengthen solidarity, but its memory will later become another object of political control.|Snowball,Boxer|sword
The windmill divides the leaders|Snowball promotes a windmill while Napoleon opposes him. Debate seems possible! Then trained dogs chase Snowball away, replacing persuasion with a visible monopoly of force.|Napoleon,Snowball|storm
A dream becomes compulsory labour|Napoleon adopts the windmill plan, demanding ever more work. Boxer answers hardship with greater effort! His devotion becomes a resource for rulers who promise more than they deliver.|Napoleon,Boxer|stone
The words change overnight|Commandments acquire convenient additions as pigs violate the original rules. Can the animals trust their own memory? Squealer's explanations make doubt feel like disloyalty.|Squealer,Benjamin|letter
Confession ends in violence|Accused animals confess and are executed. Terror enters the community! Snowball's name becomes a ready explanation for failures that the rulers will not accept responsibility for.|Napoleon,Squealer|chains
The strongest worker collapses|Boxer breaks down after years of labour. A van takes him away! Benjamin reads its slaughterhouse lettering, while Squealer supplies a story designed to conceal the betrayal.|Boxer,Benjamin,Squealer|road
The pigs become the old enemy|The rulers walk on two legs and alter the language of equality. The animals watch! What survives of a revolution when its leaders copy the privileges it opposed?|Napoleon,Squealer|crown
The faces cannot be separated|Pigs dine with humans as the others look through a window. Their likeness completes the warning! The ending asks us to remember both exploitation and the language that made it acceptable.|Napoleon,Benjamin|mirror''','How is power protected beyond physical force?','Control of language, literacy and collective memory makes the rulers’ changing privileges seem legitimate.','https://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100011h.html','All animals are equal','The final altered commandment begins with this phrase before adding its contradiction.','A shared ideal is appropriated to justify inequality.','A fable about revolutionary power and Stalinism, not a literal account of farm management.')

